Deep reinforcement learning (RL) is a promising approach for sequential decision-making, but it often requires long training durations and high computational costs. Policy-to-Dictatorship (P2D) is a new method for training a hierarchy of knowledge distillation models. It constructs a policy network that acts as a "dictator," granting sub-networks authority to decide actions based on their own predictions. P2D employs a cross entropy loss to determine when a student policy can override a teacher's action; this loss also facilitates the discovery of novel exploration methods by selecting a child network that achieves high rewards while diverging significantly from its parent. Compared to previous exploration techniques, the proposed method is more effective for entropy-aware exploration and better balances exploration and exploitation. We applied P2D to locomotion control, demonstrating that it serves as a risk-aware alternative to entropy-based exploration. Furthermore, P2D enables faster training with higher sample efficiency than standard RL.
